In modern MSTest (3.8+), you can use **constructor injection** for `TestContext` and implement `IAsyncInitialization` for async setup:

```csharp
using Microsoft.VisualStudio.TestTools.UnitTesting;

[TestClass]
public class MyTests : IAsyncInitialization
{
    public TestContext TestContext { get; }

    // Synchronous initialization via constructor
    public MyTests(TestContext testContext)
    {
        TestContext = testContext;
        // sync setup work here
    }

    // Async initialization (runs after constructor)
    public Task InitializeAsync()
    {
        // async setup work here
        return Task.CompletedTask;
    }

    [TestMethod]
    public void MyTest()
    {
        Assert.IsNotNull(TestContext);
    }
}
```

**Key points:**
- Constructor receives `TestContext` automatically via DI (MSTest 3.4+)
- `IAsyncInitialization.InitializeAsync()` runs after construction for async setup
- You can also still use `[TestInitialize]` / `[ClassInitialize]` attributes if preferred
- The classic `public TestContext TestContext { get; set; }` property injection still works too

